00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:02.840
of money for all of human history has been
debt and credit that's been recorded on ledgers,

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:08.030
on tablets, on pirates. That's been circulated
in a lot of different ways and indeed a lot

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:12.470
of the money that exists now, even though,
again, if I were to, you know, Google money,

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:17.460
the image that would come up would be a pile
of cash is actually again, ledgers, systems

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.970
of credit and debt, just that they're electronic
now.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:25.260
Yeah. So I guess when did that transition
start to happen? So certainly like a paper

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:30.540
ledger at the blacksmith shop is not digital
cash in the sense that we now sort of think

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:35.880
about it, but the idea of money being ones
and Zeros in a bank account, credit cards

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:39.930
and other forms of digital cash, how far back
does that go?

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:44.130
That goes back quite a ways actually like
a really surprising length of time. We tend

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:47.380
to think about these things as being very
recent. You know we talked about cryptocurrencies

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:52.049
as though they like fell out of the cargo
bay of a UFO like all at once, you know, a

00:01:52.049 --> 00:01:57.020
decade ago. But the history of electronic
money, like banks have been on this for decades

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:01.500
and decades and decades, right? Like they
want to have settlement systems that can work

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.820
fast, that are reliable. They already had
most of their sense of what money is in the

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.330
form of writing something down in one document
and transferring it to another document. So

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:16.120
we can see mechanisms ranging from like a
interbank settlement to cashing checks. A

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:21.239
lot of these things have been electronic for
a long time, but it was only in the 1970s

00:02:21.239 --> 00:02:26.420
that people began to realize that this kind
of money, electronic money, digital money

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:31.209
ones and Zeros, as you say, is going to be
everybody's money soon. And that that was

00:02:31.209 --> 00:02:33.570
a major social problem.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:38.680
So now I think that there's the idea that
many hold that cryptocurrency in its current

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:44.440
form is completely unique as a phenomenon.
And in a sense, I mean bitcoin is a relatively

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:51.560
new thing specifically, but some of the precedent
for bitcoin like mechanisms due date back

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:54.030
to the 70s as you're talking about, don't
they?

00:02:54.030 --> 00:03:00.709
Yes. Oh yeah, absolutely. Partially because
people, well, maybe a good way to put this

00:03:00.709 --> 00:03:05.580
is that when we talk about a technology like
Bitcoin, we're actually talking about something

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.230
that has a couple of different agendas that
it's trying to serve. And when we look at

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:14.500
where digital cash stuff came from, we basically
see those different agendas at work. So the

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:21.010
earliest forms of the underlying technologies
that eventually fed into bitcoin were digital

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:25.410
cash projects that were just designed to protect
privacy. They weren't designed to like create

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:31.430
a new form of money. They weren't designed
to, uh, necessarily let you do all of the,

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:36.460
you know, groovy new crypto currency stuff
people do now. Instead it was just a way to

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:41.330
say, I want to exchange money with you. I
want to buy something from you. I want to

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:46.510
receive a gift from you that will function
like cash so that it will create no record

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:50.980
of the transaction between us. It will not
need to know anything about our identities.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:57.209
It will not enable any form of surveillance
or control to happen for that transaction.

00:03:57.209 --> 00:04:00.720
And so that's what we first see as the idea
of digital cash should be a mechanism for

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:02.370
protecting privacy.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:07.099
When I look at the sort of genesis of the
earlier forms of digital cash and also more

00:04:07.099 --> 00:04:11.439
recently the cryptocurrency movement, it seems
that there may be, this is a little bit too

00:04:11.439 --> 00:04:18.920
simplistic, but there are sort of two political
ideologies that seem to be particularly interested.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:24.410
There's the sort of libertarian side which
is about removing the government as an intermediary,

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:31.170
privacy, uh, direct transactions rather than,
than intermediate, intermediate transactions.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:35.760
And sort of like when you look at Peter Teal
who was involved with paypal, who talked about,

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.540
well maybe paypal could even actually develop
a currency that is free from government in

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:45.680
one sense. That's the libertarian side. I
also see an, I'm curious whether you agree,

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:51.520
uh, there's the sort of like techno Utopian
side, which is just about aside from government

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:55.750
and privacy and all of these other elements.
There's the technology is just going to make

00:04:55.750 --> 00:05:01.950
everything better. Side question one is do
you agree that those are sort of the two political

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:07.300
wings that seem most interested? Number two,
are there others and is there overlap between

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.420
those two?

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:13.500
Those are excellent questions. Yeah, I think,
I think the, um, I think the answer to both

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:21.610
is yes. Um, with question one, obviously one
of the most kind of complicated legacies of

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:26.370
the way the digital cash came to be, that
still shapes a lot of aspects of how bitcoin

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:30.270
works. A lot of those technology decisions
and a lot of the kind of culture that still

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:36.690
takes place in the cryptocurrency space was
this interweaving of the libertarian side.

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:42.660
And as you say, like the techno Utopian side.
Um, and the libertarian side has its own very

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:50.000
specific way of looking at digital cash, which
is a view of the world, which is deeply suspicious

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:56.020
of the notion that the government should have
the right to mint money. Um, and that therefore

00:05:56.020 --> 00:06:04.080
we need alternative currencies that can, uh,
be as robust and reliable as something issued

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:05.080
by a central bank.

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:08.870
Like with that same level of like, I trust
that when I received this, I know what it

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:13.670
is. I can verify that it's real, but with
additional elements that are really important

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:17.990
to libertarian ideology, like the idea that
the currency should function in a way that's

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:21.550
more like a precious metal, you know, it should,
it should be issued in ways that become steadily

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:26.200
more difficult rather than mild inflation
to boost the economy. But there's also the

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:32.260
techno Utopian side, which is, you know, is
often kind of overlaps with libertarianism,

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:39.130
but which has in some ways a deeper attitude,
which is what if we could, by changing the

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:45.161
way that money works, what if we could unlock
extraordinary new levels of innovation? If

00:06:45.161 --> 00:06:50.350
we could change how markets work, if we could
change how the circulation of assets works,

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:57.980
what if that could be a spur to a whole new
level of investment and invention and transformation

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:00.190
and new businesses and new ideas.

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:05.690
And as you say, like technology plus money
would equal better, you know, produce the

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:11.450
kind of world of breakthroughs that we might
be hoping for. So those two are still by far

00:07:11.450 --> 00:07:17.080
the, the kind of most significant legacies
that shaped cryptocurrency. However, there

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:21.530
have always been lots of other interested
parties who might want to figure out how these

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:25.100
things work and they're more and more coming
to the fore and starting to shape some of

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:31.270
the future of these technologies. So in particular,
we can look at the way that a lot of marginalized

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:37.830
groups are very poorly served by the current
money system. Whether we look at people who

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:42.411
are undocumented and have to live outside
of the normal banking apparatus. If we look

00:07:42.411 --> 00:07:47.020
at people who are in poverty, really get people
who are on food stamps, like accessing and

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:50.000
interacting with cash is very expensive.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:56.230
But it's also extraordinarily difficult unless
you're a person above a certain social stratum

00:07:56.230 --> 00:08:00.810
to get any kind of access to credit or any
kind of access to normal banking systems that

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:05.330
are alternatives for that just as their systems
that could be alternatives to the incredibly

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:10.800
predatory system for sending remittances for
workers who are working overseas. Those things

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:15.800
are life changing opportunities and they have
nothing to do with that kind of libertarian,

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:22.750
excuse me. Then kind of libertarian techno
Utopian legacy. So being able to see how those

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:27.010
groups are starting to move in. I think that
shows us part of where cryptocurrency is going.

00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:31.390
Yeah, I mean I think w w you're pointing out
is interesting, which is that on the one hand,

00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:37.361
uh, there are lots of currently underserved
populations by the system that we have. At

00:08:37.361 --> 00:08:45.980
the same time, when you're talking about technology
that requires some kind of physical hardware

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:52.930
and also sort of like a wherewithal in terms
of what your, your awarenesses you. I also

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:57.520
have concerns that going cashless, at least
in the current way that it's happening in

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:01.630
many countries is also not particularly good
for some of those underserved populations.

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:05.990
So I guess the question is how to implement
something like that, a move away from cash

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:11.430
that will not particularly hamper those underserved
populations that we're sort of trying to help

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:12.740
at least in some circles.

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:17.860
Oh yeah, absolutely. And that's, that's a
really good way of looking at it too, which

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:23.530
is that there's, there's a lot of ways in
which the transition into a cashless or at

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:29.890
least less cashed society would p could be,
could be a disaster, right? Like there's,

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:34.040
there's a lot of different ways that that
could play out. There's many. Um, if you'll

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:38.410
forgive me being a little hyperbolic, there's
a lot of hungry wolves out there who have

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:45.420
been waiting for the moment when that transaction
stream, right, when the data about transactions

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:50.840
and payments finally gets folded into all
of the other surveillance data. Because the

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:56.170
moment you do that, you can start doing all
kinds of other things which are deeply discriminatory

00:09:56.170 --> 00:10:00.740
and predatory. You can start doing things
like, you know, real time price fixing and

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:04.990
red lining. We already see versions of that
